---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url.js';
---
<Base title="About" description="Engineer moving from civil and structural engineering in energy into product design and design strategy.">
  <div class="page about">
    <header>
      <p class="corner"><span class="figure-num">01</span> About</p>
      <h1 class="script about__title">About</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="about__grid">
      <div class="prose about__body">
        <p class="lede lede--small">
          I am an engineer, now studying design engineering at Harvard, after almost four
          years working across renewable energy and new-build nuclear.
        </p>

        <p>
          I studied Civil, Environmental and Geomatic Engineering at UCL and joined Mott
          MacDonald straight afterwards, working first in Seoul and then in London. The
          first couple of years were in renewable energy, reviewing feasibility-stage
          designs for offshore wind at gigawatt scale and for solar projects between 20
          and 110 MW. After that I moved onto new-build nuclear, where most of my time
          went on structural analysis and on getting engineering, safety and
          environmental evidence into a form that stakeholders could actually make
          decisions from.
        </p>

        <p>
          Looking back, the parts I was best at were rarely the calculations themselves.
          They were the translations: turning a vague client requirement into something a
          team could build against, turning a technical constraint into a decision
          someone could take, and helping peer reviewers and regulators agree on what
          counted as sufficient evidence. I did not think of it as design at the time,
          because in engineering it tends to go by another name, but that is what it was.
        </p>

        <p>
          What made me want to move was what the work was optimised for. Cost and
          programme are real constraints and I am not sentimental about them, but a
          solution can satisfy both of them and still land badly on the people who have
          to live with it. Closing that gap is rarely anyone&rsquo;s job, and I would
          rather work somewhere that it is.
        </p>

        <p>
          I am now at Harvard, on the Master in Design Engineering, which started with
          preterm in early August 2026.
        </p>

        <h2>Two baselines</h2>

        <p>
          I am a dual Korean and British citizen and I grew up in both languages, which
          turns out to be most useful as a way of comparing things. My dissertation
          started because Korean bus signage is denser and easier to scan than
          London&rsquo;s, and when I looked into why, the reason was structural rather
          than a matter of taste: Korean characters take up less horizontal space, so
          more information fits on a sign at the same legibility. Having a second
          reference point is usually how I notice that something is a problem at all.
        </p>

        <h2>Where this work stands</h2>

        <p>
          None of the projects here have shipped to real users. They sit at concept,
          analysis or draft MVP stage, and I have written them up as investigations
          rather than as products. The Patchwork MVP was put together with AI-assisted
          tools rather than hand-built, so it shows what the feature set does and not
          what I can code. I am working through CS50 separately, and this site is the
          first thing here that I built myself.
        </p>

        <p>
          My habit of defining the frame early is useful once a project is moving, and
          less useful in the divergent stage, where I can settle the question before
          there are enough options on the table to be worth settling. It is the thing I
          am most consciously working on.
        </p>

        <h2>Outside work</h2>

        <p>
          Basketball, photography and creative writing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="about__side">
        <div class="about__card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Looking for</p>
          <p>Summer 2027 internships in product design engineering, design technology, and hardware or systems design.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="about__card">
          <p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
          <p><a href="mailto:elinahsyj@gmail.com">elinahsyj@gmail.com</a></p>
          <p><a href="mailto:elina_lee@mde.harvard.edu">elina_lee@mde.harvard.edu</a></p>
          <p><a href="https://linkedin.com/in/ejl25" rel="me">linkedin.com/in/ejl25</a></p>
          <p><a href="https://github.com/shuiee" rel="me">github.com/shuiee</a></p>
        </div>
        <p><a href={href('/cv/')}>Full CV &rarr;</a></p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about { padding-top: var(--space-8); }
  .about__title {
    font-size: clamp(var(--size-8), 9vw, var(--size-10));
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-7);
  }
  .about__grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-8); }
  @media (min-width: 950px) {
    .about__grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 260px; gap: var(--space-10); }
  }
  .about__body h2 {
    font-size: var(--size-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--border-hair) solid var(--rule);
  }
  .about__side { display: grid; gap: var(--space-5); align-content: start; font-size: var(--size-2); }
  .about__card { border-top: var(--border-mark) solid var(--ink); padding-top: var(--space-3); }
  .about__card p + p { margin-top: var(--space-2); }
</style>
